<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    WHAT IS TRUE OF THIS MACHINE, and nothing else.

    It exists because two halves of gg need the same local convention and have
    nowhere to put it. Gg.Console does not reference Gg.Runner, Gg.Runner does
    not reference Gg.Client, and Gg.Contracts is the wire contract - the artifact
    a customer audits, and the package good-grief consumes from a release. A
    filesystem path is not part of a wire contract and must not ship in one.

    THE CHARTER, so this does not become a junk drawer: local paths and local
    configuration, readable by a runner and a console alike. No transport, no
    credential, no wire type. It carries no package reference and is not
    published; ProjectReferenceTests holds both.

    A second slice - browsing-work-in-the-console - reached the same conclusion
    for IntentConfiguration and a reader declaration. That is this project's
    second tenant, and the reason it is a project rather than a duplicated file.
    IT HAS NOW ARRIVED: IntentConfiguration, IntentReader, SelfInvocation,
    NominationTool's names and the work-item shapes live here, because a browse
    pane has to learn which trackers a machine reads and how to start one, and
    Gg.Console cannot reference Gg.Runner without becoming able to act as one.

    WHERE THE CHARTER GETS TESTED, said plainly so the next tenant argues rather
    than assumes. IntentReader.Locator is the closest thing here to a credential
    and it is a NAME - the string `gg credential add` files a secret under.
    Nothing in this project can resolve one, and that is the line: this carries
    which credential to use, never the credential. NominationTool.Unservable did
    NOT come, because it reads Gg.Contracts.LoopMoves and this project holds no
    project reference; it lives on ToolServers in Gg.Runner instead.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
